course: dictionary
lesson: 14777
title: Provocation: when someone tries to make you react
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A provocation is something someone does or says on purpose to make you angry or to make you react. «The boy kept calling Sam names, and it was a provocation meant to make Sam shout.» Notice the words on purpose: the other person wanted a reaction.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. «Sam did not rise to the provocation and walked away calmly.» The words did not rise tell you Sam did not get angry. Ask yourself: what was done on purpose, and what reaction was it trying to get?
explain: Provocation is a noun, so it can be one action or several. «Each provocation was a new joke, but Sam stayed calm.» The phrase without provocation means without any reason. «The argument started without provocation» means nobody gave a reason for it.
fact: Provocation means something done or said on purpose to make someone angry or to make them react.
fact: A provocation is one such action, and provocations are several of them.
fact: «Without provocation» means without any reason.
fact: Teasing and name-calling can be provocation, and you always choose how you react.
fact: Read the whole sentence to see who did something on purpose and why.
exercise: «Sam was called names all morning. The name-calling was a provocation.» What is a provocation? | A kind present for a friend | A type of playground game | *Something done or said on purpose to make someone angry | Something that happens by accident and means nothing
exercise: «The fight started without provocation.» What does without provocation mean? | *Without any reason | With a big crowd watching | After a long wait | With a loud noise
exercise: «Mia did not rise to the provocation and stayed calm.» What did Mia do? | *She did not get angry and stayed calm | She shouted back at once | She ran away crying | She started the fight
test: «The teasing was a provocation, meant to make Lily cry.» What was the teasing meant to do? | *Make Lily angry or upset | Make Lily go home early | Make Lily laugh | Make Lily fall asleep
test: «Ben replied to the slightest provocation with a shout.» What does slightest provocation suggest? | A long holiday | *The smallest reason to get angry | A big, important gift | A loud noise at night
exam: «Without provocation, the crowd turned angry, and nobody knew why.» What does without provocation mean here? | The crowd was given a very good reason | The crowd was tired and sleepy | The crowd was very large | *There was no reason for it
